-- Glasshouse: the rules of the ledger.
--
-- This file says which facts the ledger may hold and how they may
-- change. Every write is checked against it. A write that breaks a rule
-- is refused, and the refusal names the rule.
--
-- How to read it:
--   predicate       the shape of a fact (a "claim")
--   invariant       a rule that every claim must satisfy, always
--   define          a named condition (`:`) or a named figure (`=`),
--                   reused below
--   derived         a fact the ledger works out from other facts
--                   whenever it is asked, never stored
--   transformation  an act: the only way claims are added or removed
--   require         a gate: the act is refused unless this holds
--   bind            find the one claim that matches, and keep its fields
--   if / else       choose what the act does; only the branch taken runs
--   for             do the same for every claim that matches
--   admit, retract  add a claim, remove a claim
--   actor           whoever is making the proposal
--
-- Three habits run through the file:
--   * Every business fact starts with its organisation. Tenants are
--     separated by the shape of the facts, not by a filter.
--   * Permissions are claims too. They are granted by an act, so every
--     grant is on the record.
--   * Nothing is overwritten. A correction adds a new version and links
--     it to the old one. The old one stays.
--
-- Times are UTC instants. Daylight saving is handled outside the ledger.
-- A curve's prices are stored outside too. The ledger holds their hash.

program glasshouse

-- Who may do what. Each is granted by an act further down.
predicate MayCaptureTrade(actor: Subject, org: Subject, book: Subject)
predicate MayRegisterCurve(actor: Subject, org: Subject, market: Subject)
predicate MayValueTrade(actor: Subject, org: Subject, book: Subject)
predicate MaySetLimit(actor: Subject, org: Subject)
predicate MayInstruct(actor: Subject, org: Subject, book: Subject)
predicate MayApprove(actor: Subject, org: Subject, book: Subject)
-- Who may record what the market reports as filled. A reconciling
-- service, never a trader or an agent: a fill is booked even when it
-- breaks a rule, so the right to record one must be narrow.
predicate MayRecordFills(actor: Subject, org: Subject)
-- Who may answer for a breach: risk, by default the holders of
-- MaySetLimit. Never the trader behind the breach (see answer_breach).
predicate MayAnswerBreach(actor: Subject, org: Subject)
-- Who may hand out the permissions above, in one organisation.
predicate MayGrantAuthority(actor: Subject, org: Subject)
-- Who may create organisations. One holder: the ledger's root.
predicate MayEstablishOrganisations(actor: Subject)

-- Morpholog reads these two itself. Once an actor is restricted, only
-- the named database login may act as it. Anyone else is refused before
-- the rules run.
predicate ActorAssertionRestricted(actor: Subject)
predicate ActorAssertionAuthority(actor: Subject, login_role: Subject)

-- Who may roll an organisation's business date.
predicate MayRollBusinessDate(actor: Subject, org: Subject)

-- The organisation's business date: the day its valuations are for. One
-- per organisation at a time, moved forward by an act (never back), so
-- every roll is in the audit history and "which day were we valuing
-- last Tuesday at 10:00" has one answer. It is a date the desk rolls,
-- not the clock: the clock is `decided_at`, and the two are never mixed.
predicate BusinessDate(org: Subject, date: Date)
    unique by (org)

-- The currency a market's prices and valuations are in. Set once, when
-- the market is declared, and never changed. This is the one place a
-- currency comes from: a trade or a curve takes its market's.
-- A currency is a field here, not a unit like MW, because a unit is
-- fixed for a whole predicate and a currency differs from market to
-- market.
predicate MarketCurrency(org: Subject, market: Subject, currency: Subject)
    unique by (org, market)
    append only

-- A trade's identity. It never changes. Direction and counterparty are
-- part of what the trade is.
predicate TradeCaptured(org: Subject, book: Subject, trade: Subject, counterparty: Subject, market: Subject, direction: Subject)
    unique by (trade)
    append only

-- A trade's economic terms, one claim per version. An amendment adds a
-- version. `effective by` gives us `trade_terms_in_force_on`: the
-- version in force for a trade on a date. `partial` allows dates with
-- no version (before the trade date).
-- The price is per MWh, in the currency of the trade's market (see
-- MarketCurrency). It is a ratio, so it carries no unit here.
predicate TradeTerms(org: Subject, trade: Subject, version: Subject, quantity: Decimal[MW], price: Decimal, delivery_start: Timestamp, delivery_end: Timestamp, effective_from: Date)
    unique by (version)
    effective by (trade) on (effective_from) partial
    append only

-- Which terms version replaced which. A version is replaced at most once.
predicate TradeTermsSupersedes(new_version: Subject, prior_version: Subject)
    unique by (prior_version)
    append only

-- One version of a price curve for a market and date. The prices live
-- outside the ledger. `payload_hash` pins them.
predicate CurveRegistered(org: Subject, market: Subject, as_of: Date, version: Subject, payload_hash: Subject)
    unique by (version)
    append only

-- The curve version to use for a market and date. Exactly one at a time.
-- A correction moves the pointer and records what it replaced.
predicate OfficialCurve(org: Subject, market: Subject, as_of: Date, version: Subject)
    current pointer by (org, market, as_of)
    superseded via CurveSupersedes
predicate CurveSupersedes(new_version: Subject, prior_version: Subject)
    append only

-- One mark-to-market result. It names the exact curve version and terms
-- version it was computed from, so a later correction never changes a
-- number someone already saw. It states its currency, which must be
-- the currency of the trade's market.
predicate TradeValued(org: Subject, book: Subject, trade: Subject, curve_version: Subject, terms_version: Subject, mtm: Decimal, currency: Subject)
    unique by (trade, curve_version, terms_version)
    append only

-- A book's cap on its net position (bought less sold) in any one hour.
-- One cap per book. Not append only: a new cap replaces the old one, and
-- the audit log keeps every cap that ever stood.
predicate PositionLimit(org: Subject, book: Subject, cap: Decimal[MW])
    unique by (org, book)

-- An instruction: someone intends to buy or sell, and has not done so
-- yet. An order sent to market and not yet filled is one. This claim is
-- what was intended. It never changes.
-- The limit price is per MWh in the currency of the market.
predicate Instruction(org: Subject, book: Subject, instruction: Subject, proposer: Subject, market: Subject, direction: Subject, quantity: Decimal[MW], limit_price: Decimal, delivery_start: Timestamp, delivery_end: Timestamp, placed_on: Date, valid_until: Timestamp)
    unique by (instruction)
    append only

-- What an instruction still holds. While this claim stands and its
-- `valid_until` has not passed, the quantity counts against the book's
-- position limit, so nobody else can use that room. It is removed when
-- the instruction ends. Not append only for that reason.
predicate InstructionOpen(org: Subject, book: Subject, instruction: Subject, direction: Subject, remaining: Decimal[MW], delivery_start: Timestamp, delivery_end: Timestamp, valid_until: Timestamp)
    unique by (instruction)

-- A book whose instructions need a second person. Declared once per
-- book. In such a book an instruction should not be acted on until it
-- is approved. The ledger cannot stop an order leaving the building: it
-- records whether approval was given, and by whom.
predicate ApprovalRequired(org: Subject, book: Subject)
    append only

-- An instruction's approval. Given once, by someone other than whoever
-- placed it.
predicate InstructionApproved(org: Subject, instruction: Subject, approver: Subject)
    unique by (instruction)
    append only

-- How an instruction ended. Recorded once.
predicate InstructionEnded(org: Subject, instruction: Subject, outcome: Subject)
    unique by (instruction)
    append only

-- A fill the market reported against an instruction, and the trade it
-- was booked as. `execution` is the outside firm's own identifier for
-- the fill, and both identities are unique: the same report is booked
-- once, whatever trade id a second attempt offers, even when two
-- attempts race.
predicate InstructionFill(org: Subject, instruction: Subject, execution: Subject, trade: Subject)
    unique by (trade)
    unique by (org, execution)
    append only

-- Something that happened and should not have. The trade stands: it
-- happened. The breach is what someone must answer for. The kinds:
--   #limit       the fill took the book past its cap in some hour
--   #unapproved  the book requires approval and nobody approved
--   #after_end   the instruction had already ended (a late fill)
--   #price       a worse price than the instruction's limit price
--   #overfill    more than the instruction still held
predicate Breach(org: Subject, book: Subject, trade: Subject, kind: Subject)
    unique by (trade, kind)
    append only

-- Who looked at a breach, what they decided, and why. Once per breach.
-- The decision is one of a closed list, so it can be reported on; the
-- note says why in words. An answer records a decision, not that it was
-- carried out: `#unwind` does not mean the trade was unwound, nor
-- `#cap_raised` that the cap was changed. That is why the open read is
-- named for what it knows, unanswered, not unresolved.
predicate BreachAnswered(org: Subject, trade: Subject, kind: Subject, answered_by: Subject, decision: Subject, note: Subject)
    unique by (trade, kind)
    append only

-- Rules that hold for every claim, whichever act wrote it.
-- (Uniqueness, the single official pointer and the version chains are
-- generated from the clauses on the predicates above.)
--
-- A note for editors: a rule is re-checked for the claims its pattern
-- names. Name the claim's unique key (`trade`, `version`) even when the
-- rule does not use it. Then a write re-checks one claim, not every
-- claim that shares a value, and concurrent writers do not collide.

-- The currencies European power markets are priced in. A closed list,
-- so a mistyped code cannot become a market's currency for good.
-- Adding one is a change to this file.
invariant currency_is_one_power_is_priced_in:
    MarketCurrency(org: org, market: market, currency: currency)
    implies currency in (#EUR, #GBP, #NOK, #SEK, #DKK, #PLN, #RON)

invariant direction_is_buy_or_sell:
    TradeCaptured(trade: trade, direction: direction, ..)
    implies direction in (#buy, #sell)

invariant position_limit_is_positive:
    PositionLimit(org: org, book: book, cap: cap) implies cap > 0 MW

invariant instruction_is_buy_or_sell:
    Instruction(instruction: instruction, direction: direction, ..)
    implies direction in (#buy, #sell)

invariant instructed_quantity_is_positive:
    Instruction(instruction: instruction, quantity: qty, ..) implies qty > 0 MW

invariant open_quantity_is_positive:
    InstructionOpen(instruction: instruction, remaining: left, ..) implies left > 0 MW

invariant open_instruction_was_placed:
    InstructionOpen(org: org, instruction: instruction, ..)
    implies Instruction(org: org, instruction: instruction, ..)

invariant approved_instruction_was_placed:
    InstructionApproved(org: org, instruction: instruction, ..)
    implies Instruction(org: org, instruction: instruction, ..)

invariant instruction_ends_one_of_four_ways:
    InstructionEnded(instruction: instruction, outcome: outcome, ..)
    implies outcome in (#filled, #rejected, #cancelled, #lapsed)

invariant fill_was_instructed:
    InstructionFill(org: org, instruction: instruction, trade: trade, ..)
    implies Instruction(org: org, instruction: instruction, ..)

invariant fill_was_booked:
    InstructionFill(org: org, trade: trade, ..) implies TradeCaptured(org: org, trade: trade, ..)

invariant breach_is_of_a_known_kind:
    Breach(trade: trade, kind: kind, ..) implies kind in (#limit, #unapproved, #after_end, #price, #overfill)

invariant breach_decision_is_one_of_four:
    BreachAnswered(trade: trade, kind: kind, decision: decision, ..)
    implies decision in (#accepted, #unwind, #cap_raised, #disputed)

invariant breach_names_a_booked_trade:
    Breach(org: org, book: book, trade: trade, kind: kind)
    implies TradeCaptured(org: org, book: book, trade: trade, ..)

invariant answer_names_a_recorded_breach:
    BreachAnswered(org: org, trade: trade, kind: kind, ..)
    implies Breach(org: org, trade: trade, kind: kind, ..)

invariant terms_belong_to_captured_trade:
    TradeTerms(org: org, trade: trade, ..) implies TradeCaptured(org: org, trade: trade, ..)

invariant quantity_is_positive:
    TradeTerms(trade: trade, version: version, quantity: qty, ..) implies qty > 0 MW

invariant delivery_period_is_ordered:
    TradeTerms(trade: trade, version: version, delivery_start: delivery_start, delivery_end: delivery_end, ..)
    implies delivery_start strictly_before delivery_end

-- The official pointer names a version registered for that same
-- organisation, market and date.
invariant official_curve_is_registered:
    OfficialCurve(org, market, as_of, version)
    implies CurveRegistered(org: org, market: market, as_of: as_of, version: version, ..)

invariant valued_trade_was_captured:
    TradeValued(org: org, book: book, trade: trade, ..)
    implies TradeCaptured(org: org, book: book, trade: trade, ..)

invariant valuation_curve_is_registered:
    TradeValued(org: org, curve_version: version, ..)
    implies CurveRegistered(org: org, version: version, ..)

invariant valuation_terms_version_is_registered:
    TradeValued(org: org, trade: trade, terms_version: version, ..)
    implies TradeTerms(org: org, trade: trade, version: version, ..)

-- A version replaces a version of the same trade.
invariant terms_lineage_stays_within_the_trade:
    TradeTermsSupersedes(new_version, prior_version)
    and TradeTerms(org: org, trade: trade, version: new_version, ..)
    implies TradeTerms(org: org, trade: trade, version: prior_version, ..)

-- Deliberately not rules: "a mark uses the official curve" and "a mark
-- uses the terms in force". Both are checked once, when the mark is
-- admitted (see admit_valuation). A mark records what was computed
-- then. A later correction or amendment must not make it unlawful.

-- Named conditions.

-- The terms in force on a date, for every trade of one organisation.
-- It picks the same version as the generated `trade_terms_in_force_on`.
-- The difference is the key. The generated one is looked up by trade.
-- The limit check finds its trades as it runs, so through the generated
-- one it would read every organisation's terms, and two organisations
-- capturing at the same moment would collide. This one is looked up by
-- organisation, which the act knows from the start.
define org_terms_in_force_on(org, as_of, trade, quantity, delivery_start, delivery_end):
    TradeTerms(org: org, trade: trade, quantity: quantity, delivery_start: delivery_start, delivery_end: delivery_end, effective_from: from, ..)
    and from on_or_before as_of
    and not (exists later: TradeTerms(org: org, trade: trade, effective_from: later, ..) and later after from and later on_or_before as_of)

-- How much the book has booked on one side (`#buy` or `#sell`) for the
-- hour starting at `h`, under the terms in force on `as_of`. The trade
-- named `excluded` is left out: an amendment replaces its own earlier
-- quantity. A new trade is not in the book yet, so naming it changes
-- nothing.
define booked_in_hour(org, book, as_of, excluded, side, h) = (sum(q | org_terms_in_force_on(org, as_of, t, q, s, e)
        and TradeCaptured(org: org, book: book, trade: t, direction: side, ..)
        and t != excluded and s at_or_before h and h strictly_before e))

-- The book's net position in that hour: bought less sold, leaving out
-- the trade named `excluded`.
define net_in_hour(org, book, as_of, excluded, h) = (booked_in_hour(org, book, as_of, excluded, #buy, h)
    - booked_in_hour(org, book, as_of, excluded, #sell, h))

-- One trade's own signed quantity in that hour (bought positive, sold
-- negative), read by the trade: zero for a trade not booked yet.
define trade_in_hour(org, as_of, trade, h) = (sum(q | org_terms_in_force_on(org, as_of, trade, q, s, e)
        and TradeCaptured(org: org, trade: trade, direction: #buy, ..)
        and s at_or_before h and h strictly_before e)
    - sum(q | org_terms_in_force_on(org, as_of, trade, q, s, e)
        and TradeCaptured(org: org, trade: trade, direction: #sell, ..)
        and s at_or_before h and h strictly_before e))

-- Where the hour stands before the act: the net with every trade
-- counted, the excluded one too.
define standing_in_hour(org, book, as_of, excluded, h) = (net_in_hour(org, book, as_of, excluded, h)
    + trade_in_hour(org, as_of, excluded, h))

-- What open instructions on one side still hold in that hour, at the
-- instant `now`: one past its `valid_until` holds nothing.
define held_in_hour(org, book, side, h, now) = (sum(left | InstructionOpen(org: org, book: book, instruction: i, direction: side, remaining: left, delivery_start: s, delivery_end: e, valid_until: until)
        and s at_or_before h and h strictly_before e and now at_or_before until))

-- Does the book keep to its cap in hour `h` once the proposed quantity
-- (negative for a sale) is added? The net must stay within the cap, or,
-- where the hour already stands further out than the cap (a fill can
-- take it there), no further out than it stands. That is how a book
-- over its cap trades its way back: towards flat, never further out.
-- It is written as one comparison so that a refusal reports its two
-- figures ("26 MW <= 25 MW does not hold"). Within the cap that reads
-- exactly as a plain cap check.
define hour_is_within_cap(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, h, cap):
    let after = (net_in_hour(org, book, as_of, excluded, h) + signed_quantity)
    let before = (standing_in_hour(org, book, as_of, excluded, h))
    abs(after) <= max(cap, abs(before))

-- The rest of the way back from a breach: in an hour already over its
-- cap, the act must make the net strictly smaller. The comparison above
-- lets the net stay as far out as it stands, so without this a book in
-- breach could flip from +120 to -120 and back, each trade admitted and
-- none of them reducing anything. Within the cap this always holds.
-- `excluded` and `signed_quantity` are as above; for an instruction the
-- signed quantity is the instruction itself, as if it filled.
define breach_shrinks(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, h, cap):
    let after = (net_in_hour(org, book, as_of, excluded, h) + signed_quantity)
    let before = (standing_in_hour(org, book, as_of, excluded, h))
    abs(before) <= cap or abs(after) < abs(before)

-- Is there still room in hour `h` once open instructions are counted?
-- An open instruction may fill or may not, so it is never set against
-- the other side. Open buys count as if they will all fill, towards
-- the bought side. Open sells count the same way towards the sold side.
-- Each side must stay within the cap or, where the hour already stands
-- further out than the cap (its larger side, every open instruction
-- counted as filled), no further out than that: the same way back from
-- a breach as above.
-- The figures a refusal reports first are: the quantity instructed for
-- that side (open instructions plus the one being placed), and the room
-- the booked trades leave on that side. So "7 MW <= 5 MW does not hold"
-- reads: 7 MW is instructed and only 5 MW of room is left.
-- An instruction holds its room until it ends (filled, cancelled or
-- rejected) or until its `valid_until` passes, whichever comes first:
-- `now` is the act's decision time, so an instruction past its time
-- stops holding room by this rule, before any sweep records its lapse.
-- A preview ("would this be accepted?") is judged at the instant it is
-- asked, so an instruction can run out between a preview and the act.
-- `excluded` and `signed_quantity` are as in hour_is_within_cap.
-- `more_bought` and `more_sold` are the instruction being placed.
define hour_has_room_for_instructions(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, more_bought, more_sold, h, cap, now):
    let after = (net_in_hour(org, book, as_of, excluded, h) + signed_quantity)
    let before = (standing_in_hour(org, book, as_of, excluded, h))
    let bought = (held_in_hour(org, book, #buy, h, now))
    let sold = (held_in_hour(org, book, #sell, h, now))
    let exposure = (max(bought + before, sold - before))
    more_bought + bought <= max(cap, exposure) - after
    and more_sold + sold <= max(cap, exposure) + after

-- The three limit rules, checked over a whole delivery window. A
-- book's net position, and what its open instructions hold, change only
-- where some trade or instruction in the book starts or ends. So each
-- rule is checked at the window's first hour and at every such start
-- and end inside the window: that proves it for every hour, without
-- adding up the book once for each of the up to 8784 hours. A refusal
-- names the hour, the start of the stretch that breaks the rule.
define keeps_to_cap(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, delivery_start, delivery_end, cap):
    hour_is_within_cap(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, delivery_start, cap)
    and (forall h in TradeTerms(org: org, trade: t, delivery_start: h, ..):
        (TradeCaptured(org: org, book: book, trade: t, ..) and delivery_start strictly_before h and h strictly_before delivery_end)
        implies hour_is_within_cap(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, h, cap))
    and (forall h in TradeTerms(org: org, trade: t, delivery_end: h, ..):
        (TradeCaptured(org: org, book: book, trade: t, ..) and delivery_start strictly_before h and h strictly_before delivery_end)
        implies hour_is_within_cap(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, h, cap))

define shrinks_where_breached(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, delivery_start, delivery_end, cap):
    breach_shrinks(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, delivery_start, cap)
    and (forall h in TradeTerms(org: org, trade: t, delivery_start: h, ..):
        (TradeCaptured(org: org, book: book, trade: t, ..) and delivery_start strictly_before h and h strictly_before delivery_end)
        implies breach_shrinks(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, h, cap))
    and (forall h in TradeTerms(org: org, trade: t, delivery_end: h, ..):
        (TradeCaptured(org: org, book: book, trade: t, ..) and delivery_start strictly_before h and h strictly_before delivery_end)
        implies breach_shrinks(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, h, cap))

define has_room_at_breakpoints(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, more_bought, more_sold, delivery_start, delivery_end, cap, now):
    hour_has_room_for_instructions(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, more_bought, more_sold, delivery_start, cap, now)
    and (forall h in TradeTerms(org: org, trade: t, delivery_start: h, ..):
        (TradeCaptured(org: org, book: book, trade: t, ..) and delivery_start strictly_before h and h strictly_before delivery_end)
        implies hour_has_room_for_instructions(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, more_bought, more_sold, h, cap, now))
    and (forall h in TradeTerms(org: org, trade: t, delivery_end: h, ..):
        (TradeCaptured(org: org, book: book, trade: t, ..) and delivery_start strictly_before h and h strictly_before delivery_end)
        implies hour_has_room_for_instructions(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, more_bought, more_sold, h, cap, now))
    and (forall h in InstructionOpen(org: org, book: book, delivery_start: h, ..):
        (delivery_start strictly_before h and h strictly_before delivery_end)
        implies hour_has_room_for_instructions(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, more_bought, more_sold, h, cap, now))
    and (forall h in InstructionOpen(org: org, book: book, delivery_end: h, ..):
        (delivery_start strictly_before h and h strictly_before delivery_end)
        implies hour_has_room_for_instructions(org, book, as_of, excluded, signed_quantity, more_bought, more_sold, h, cap, now))

-- Where each instruction stands, as the Positions screen, the API and
-- the agent list it: what was intended, what it still holds under the
-- limit, how it ended (or `#open`), and whether a second person has
-- agreed. One read, worked out by the ledger, so the facts it comes
-- from always agree with each other and it can be asked of any past
-- moment. Whether an open instruction is past its time is the reader's
-- clock, not the ledger's, so it is not here.
predicate InstructionStanding(org: Subject, instruction: Subject, book: Subject, market: Subject, direction: Subject, quantity: Decimal[MW], limit_price: Decimal, currency: Subject, delivery_start: Timestamp, delivery_end: Timestamp, placed_on: Date, valid_until: Timestamp, proposer: Subject, holding: Decimal[MW], outcome: Subject, approval: Subject, approved_by: Subject)
derived InstructionStanding(org, instruction, book, market):
    over Instruction(org: org, instruction: instruction, book: book, market: market, ..)
    value direction = value Instruction(org: org, instruction: instruction, direction: _, ..)
    value quantity = value Instruction(org: org, instruction: instruction, quantity: _, ..)
    value limit_price = value Instruction(org: org, instruction: instruction, limit_price: _, ..)
    value currency = value MarketCurrency(org: org, market: market, currency: _)
    value delivery_start = value Instruction(org: org, instruction: instruction, delivery_start: _, ..)
    value delivery_end = value Instruction(org: org, instruction: instruction, delivery_end: _, ..)
    value placed_on = value Instruction(org: org, instruction: instruction, placed_on: _, ..)
    value valid_until = value Instruction(org: org, instruction: instruction, valid_until: _, ..)
    value proposer = value Instruction(org: org, instruction: instruction, proposer: _, ..)
    value holding = value InstructionOpen(org: org, instruction: instruction, remaining: _, ..) default 0 MW
    value outcome = value InstructionEnded(org: org, instruction: instruction, outcome: _) default #open
    value approval = if(InstructionApproved(org: org, instruction: instruction, ..), #approved, if(ApprovalRequired(org: org, book: book), #awaiting, #not_required))
    value approved_by = value InstructionApproved(org: org, instruction: instruction, approver: _) default #nobody

-- What a trade is valued against on the organisation's business date:
-- the official curve for that day in its market, and its terms in force
-- on that day. One definition, so the per-trade read below and the
-- totals after it can never pick different inputs.
define valued_against_on_business_date(org, trade, market, business_date, curve_version, terms_version):
    TradeCaptured(org: org, trade: trade, market: market, ..)
    and BusinessDate(org: org, date: business_date)
    and OfficialCurve(org: org, market: market, as_of: business_date, version: curve_version)
    and trade_terms_in_force_on(trade: trade, as_of: business_date, org: org, version: terms_version, ..)

-- A mark struck for the business date: against exactly those inputs, in
-- the market's currency.
define marked_on_business_date(org, trade, business_date, currency, mtm):
    valued_against_on_business_date(org, trade, market, business_date, curve_version, terms_version)
    and TradeValued(org: org, trade: trade, curve_version: curve_version, terms_version: terms_version, mtm: mtm, ..)
    and MarketCurrency(org: org, market: market, currency: currency)

-- Each trade's valuation for the organisation's business date: the
-- official curve for that day in the trade's market, the terms in force
-- on it, and whether a mark has been struck against those two yet (and
-- for how much, in the market's currency). After a roll, a trade valued
-- only on an earlier day reads `valued = false` here, so a screen never
-- shows yesterday's number as today's. A trade whose market has no
-- official curve for the day has no row.
predicate ValuationOnBusinessDate(org: Subject, trade: Subject, market: Subject, business_date: Date, curve_version: Subject, terms_version: Subject, valued: Bool, mtm: Decimal, currency: Subject)
derived ValuationOnBusinessDate(org, trade, market, business_date, curve_version, terms_version):
    over valued_against_on_business_date(org, trade, market, business_date, curve_version, terms_version)
    value valued = if(TradeValued(org: org, trade: trade, curve_version: curve_version, terms_version: terms_version, ..), true, false)
    value mtm = value TradeValued(org: org, trade: trade, curve_version: curve_version, terms_version: terms_version, mtm: _, ..) default 0
    value currency = value MarketCurrency(org: org, market: market, currency: _)

-- What the organisation's trades are worth on its business date, one
-- total per currency: the marks struck for that day, never a sum across
-- currencies and never an earlier day's mark. This is the figure the
-- Overview shows, and the one an audit receipt states, so the ledger,
-- not the screen, is what adds it up. `valued` counts the marks in it.
predicate BusinessDayTotal(org: Subject, business_date: Date, currency: Subject, total: Decimal, valued: Decimal)
derived BusinessDayTotal(org, business_date, currency):
    over marked_on_business_date(org, _, business_date, currency, _)
    value total = sum(m | marked_on_business_date(org, t, business_date, currency, m))
    value valued = sum(1 | marked_on_business_date(org, t, business_date, currency, m))

-- Each trade that cannot be valued for the business date, and why: no
-- official curve for that day in its market (`#missing_curve`), or no
-- terms in force on it yet (`#no_terms_in_force`, a trade that starts
-- later). Together with ValuationOnBusinessDate this covers every
-- captured trade, so a trade never drops out of the count because what
-- is needed to value it is missing.
predicate ValuationGap(org: Subject, trade: Subject, market: Subject, business_date: Date, reason: Subject)
derived ValuationGap(org, trade, market, business_date):
    over TradeCaptured(org: org, trade: trade, market: market, ..)
    and BusinessDate(org: org, date: business_date)
    and not (OfficialCurve(org: org, market: market, as_of: business_date, ..)
        and trade_terms_in_force_on(trade: trade, as_of: business_date, org: org, ..))
    value reason = if(OfficialCurve(org: org, market: market, as_of: business_date, ..), #no_terms_in_force, #missing_curve)

-- The breaches nobody has answered yet, with the fill each came from:
-- what the Positions screen lists. Worked out by the ledger each time
-- it is asked, so it can never disagree with the facts it is read from.
-- It computes nothing: its rows are the breaches that meet the condition
-- (a fill is unique by trade, so each breach has one execution), which
-- also lets it be a live SQL view.
predicate UnansweredBreach(org: Subject, book: Subject, trade: Subject, kind: Subject, execution: Subject)
derived UnansweredBreach(org, book, trade, kind, execution):
    over Breach(org: org, book: book, trade: trade, kind: kind) and not BreachAnswered(org: org, trade: trade, kind: kind, ..)
    and InstructionFill(org: org, trade: trade, execution: execution, ..)

-- The acts. Every gate has a name, and a refusal reports that name.

-- Runs once, when the database is provisioned. The caller becomes the
-- root: it may create organisations, and only its own database login
-- may act as it from then on.
transformation establish_ledger(login_role):
    require ledger_is_new: not MayEstablishOrganisations(..)
    admit MayEstablishOrganisations(actor)
    admit ActorAssertionRestricted(actor)
    admit ActorAssertionAuthority(actor, login_role)

-- Create an organisation. The root can grant permissions in it.
transformation establish_organisation(org):
    require may_establish_organisations: MayEstablishOrganisations(actor)
    require organisation_is_new: not MayGrantAuthority(org: org, ..)
    admit MayGrantAuthority(actor, org)

-- Let someone else grant permissions in an organisation. They are tied
-- to one database login in the same act. Otherwise anyone could claim
-- to be them and grant themselves anything.
transformation grant_grant_authority(principal, org, login_role):
    require may_grant_authority: MayGrantAuthority(actor, org)
    admit MayGrantAuthority(principal, org)
    admit ActorAssertionRestricted(principal)
    admit ActorAssertionAuthority(principal, login_role)

transformation grant_capture_authority(principal, org, book):
    require may_grant_authority: MayGrantAuthority(actor, org)
    admit MayCaptureTrade(principal, org, book)

transformation grant_curve_authority(principal, org, market):
    require may_grant_authority: MayGrantAuthority(actor, org)
    admit MayRegisterCurve(principal, org, market)

transformation grant_valuation_authority(principal, org, book):
    require may_grant_authority: MayGrantAuthority(actor, org)
    admit MayValueTrade(principal, org, book)

transformation grant_instruct_authority(principal, org, book):
    require may_grant_authority: MayGrantAuthority(actor, org)
    admit MayInstruct(principal, org, book)

transformation grant_approve_authority(principal, org, book):
    require may_grant_authority: MayGrantAuthority(actor, org)
    admit MayApprove(principal, org, book)

transformation grant_fill_authority(principal, org):
    require may_grant_authority: MayGrantAuthority(actor, org)
    admit MayRecordFills(principal, org)

transformation grant_breach_authority(principal, org):
    require may_grant_authority: MayGrantAuthority(actor, org)
    admit MayAnswerBreach(principal, org)

-- Say that a book's instructions need approval from now on.
transformation require_approval(org, book):
    require may_grant_authority: MayGrantAuthority(actor, org)
    require approval_not_already_required: not ApprovalRequired(org: org, book: book)
    admit ApprovalRequired(org, book)

transformation grant_limit_authority(principal, org):
    require may_grant_authority: MayGrantAuthority(actor, org)
    admit MaySetLimit(principal, org)

transformation grant_date_authority(principal, org):
    require may_grant_authority: MayGrantAuthority(actor, org)
    admit MayRollBusinessDate(principal, org)

-- Roll the organisation's business date to `date`, later than the one it
-- replaces (the first can be any date). From then on a valuation uses the
-- official curve for that date. The days rolled past keep their curves
-- and their marks; nothing about them changes.
transformation roll_business_date(org, date):
    require may_roll_business_date: MayRollBusinessDate(actor, org)
    require date_moves_forward: (forall current in BusinessDate(org, current): date after current)
    retract BusinessDate(org: org, ..)
    admit BusinessDate(org, date)

-- Declare a market and the currency it is priced in. Done once per
-- market, by whoever may grant permissions in the organisation. Trades
-- and curves in a market are refused until it is declared.
transformation declare_market(org, market, currency):
    require may_grant_authority: MayGrantAuthority(actor, org)
    require market_is_new: not MarketCurrency(org: org, market: market, ..)
    admit MarketCurrency(org, market, currency)

-- Set a book's cap, or replace the one it has. The retract removes the
-- standing cap if there is one and does nothing if there is none.
-- A book with no cap is uncapped. Lowering a cap does not undo trades
-- already in the book. It only limits the next one.
transformation set_position_limit(org, book, cap):
    require may_set_limit: MaySetLimit(actor, org)
    retract PositionLimit(org: org, book: book, ..)
    admit PositionLimit(org, book, cap)

-- Capture a trade: its identity and its first terms, in force from the
-- trade date.
-- The delivery window must run forwards, last at most a year (8784
-- hours, a leap year) and be whole hours. `hours` is the start of every
-- hour the window touches. A whole-hour window touches exactly as many
-- hours as it is long, so a trade starting at half past is refused.
-- Then the cap is checked in each of those hours, twice. First against
-- the booked trades alone (hour_within_limit). Then counting the room
-- open instructions hold (room_not_held_by_instructions). The name of
-- the gate that refuses says which it was. With no cap set, both pass.
transformation capture_trade(org, book, trade, counterparty, market, direction, version, quantity, price, delivery_start, delivery_end, trade_date):
    require may_capture_trade: MayCaptureTrade(actor, org, book)
    require market_has_a_currency: MarketCurrency(org: org, market: market, ..)
    require trade_is_new: not TradeCaptured(trade: trade, ..)
    require terms_version_is_new: not TradeTerms(version: version, ..)
    require delivery_is_forward: delivery_start strictly_before delivery_end
    require delivery_is_at_most_a_year: delivery_end - delivery_start no_longer_than duration(PT8784H)
    let hours = periods(delivery_start, delivery_end, duration(PT1H))
    require delivery_is_whole_hours: (delivery_end - delivery_start) / duration(PT1H) = sum(1 | h in hours)
    let signed = if(direction = #buy, quantity, 0 MW - quantity)
    let now = decided_at
    require hour_within_limit: (forall cap in PositionLimit(org, book, cap):
        keeps_to_cap(org, book, trade_date, trade, signed, delivery_start, delivery_end, cap))
    require breach_must_shrink: (forall cap in PositionLimit(org, book, cap):
        shrinks_where_breached(org, book, trade_date, trade, signed, delivery_start, delivery_end, cap))
    require room_not_held_by_instructions: (forall cap in PositionLimit(org, book, cap):
        has_room_at_breakpoints(org, book, trade_date, trade, signed, 0 MW, 0 MW, delivery_start, delivery_end, cap, now))
    admit TradeCaptured(org, book, trade, counterparty, market, direction)
    admit TradeTerms(org, trade, version, quantity, price, delivery_start, delivery_end, trade_date)

-- Amend a trade's terms. The new version sits beside the old one and
-- takes effect on a later date. Only the latest version can be amended.
-- Whoever may capture in the book may amend in it. The window and the
-- cap are checked as at capture.
transformation amend_trade(org, trade, prior_version, new_version, quantity, price, delivery_start, delivery_end, effective_from):
    bind trade_is_captured: TradeCaptured(org, book, trade, _, _, direction)
    require may_amend_trade: MayCaptureTrade(actor, org, book)
    require amendment_takes_effect_later: TradeTerms(org: org, trade: trade, version: prior_version, effective_from: prior_from, ..)
    and effective_from after prior_from
    require prior_terms_are_the_latest: not TradeTermsSupersedes(prior_version: prior_version, ..)
    require terms_version_is_new: not TradeTerms(version: new_version, ..)
    require delivery_is_forward: delivery_start strictly_before delivery_end
    require delivery_is_at_most_a_year: delivery_end - delivery_start no_longer_than duration(PT8784H)
    let hours = periods(delivery_start, delivery_end, duration(PT1H))
    require delivery_is_whole_hours: (delivery_end - delivery_start) / duration(PT1H) = sum(1 | h in hours)
    let signed = if(direction = #buy, quantity, 0 MW - quantity)
    let now = decided_at
    require hour_within_limit: (forall cap in PositionLimit(org, book, cap):
        keeps_to_cap(org, book, effective_from, trade, signed, delivery_start, delivery_end, cap))
    require breach_must_shrink: (forall cap in PositionLimit(org, book, cap):
        shrinks_where_breached(org, book, effective_from, trade, signed, delivery_start, delivery_end, cap))
    require room_not_held_by_instructions: (forall cap in PositionLimit(org, book, cap):
        has_room_at_breakpoints(org, book, effective_from, trade, signed, 0 MW, 0 MW, delivery_start, delivery_end, cap, now))
    admit TradeTerms(org, trade, new_version, quantity, price, delivery_start, delivery_end, effective_from)
    admit TradeTermsSupersedes(new_version, prior_version)

-- Place an instruction: an intention to buy or sell that has not
-- happened yet. From this moment it holds room under the book's limit,
-- so a trade or another instruction cannot take that room. The window
-- is checked as for a trade. Approval, where a book requires it, is a
-- separate act by a different person (approve_instruction). It is not
-- yet connected to fills: that rule comes later.
transformation place_instruction(org, book, instruction, market, direction, quantity, limit_price, delivery_start, delivery_end, placed_on, valid_until):
    require may_instruct: MayInstruct(actor, org, book)
    require market_has_a_currency: MarketCurrency(org: org, market: market, ..)
    require instruction_is_new: not Instruction(instruction: instruction, ..)
    let now = decided_at
    require instruction_is_still_valid: now at_or_before valid_until
    require delivery_is_forward: delivery_start strictly_before delivery_end
    require delivery_is_at_most_a_year: delivery_end - delivery_start no_longer_than duration(PT8784H)
    let hours = periods(delivery_start, delivery_end, duration(PT1H))
    require delivery_is_whole_hours: (delivery_end - delivery_start) / duration(PT1H) = sum(1 | h in hours)
    let bought = if(direction = #buy, quantity, 0 MW)
    let sold = if(direction = #sell, quantity, 0 MW)
    let as_if_filled = bought - sold
    require breach_must_shrink: (forall cap in PositionLimit(org, book, cap):
        shrinks_where_breached(org, book, placed_on, instruction, as_if_filled, delivery_start, delivery_end, cap))
    require instruction_fits_under_limit: (forall cap in PositionLimit(org, book, cap):
        has_room_at_breakpoints(org, book, placed_on, instruction, 0 MW, bought, sold, delivery_start, delivery_end, cap, now))
    admit Instruction(org, book, instruction, actor, market, direction, quantity, limit_price, delivery_start, delivery_end, placed_on, valid_until)
    admit InstructionOpen(org, book, instruction, direction, quantity, delivery_start, delivery_end, valid_until)

-- Cancel an open instruction. The room it held is free again at once.
-- Whoever may instruct in the book may cancel in it.
transformation cancel_instruction(org, instruction):
    bind instruction_is_open: InstructionOpen(org: org, book: book, instruction: instruction, ..)
    require may_instruct: MayInstruct(actor, org, book)
    retract InstructionOpen(org: org, instruction: instruction, ..)
    admit InstructionEnded(org, instruction, #cancelled)

-- Approve an open instruction. The approver must hold the right in the
-- instruction's book and must not be the person who placed it: a second
-- pair of eyes is a different pair. An instruction cannot change after
-- it is placed, and it has held its room since then, so an approval
-- never goes out of date.
transformation approve_instruction(org, instruction):
    bind instruction_is_open: InstructionOpen(org: org, book: book, instruction: instruction, ..)
    bind instruction_was_placed: Instruction(org: org, instruction: instruction, proposer: proposer, ..)
    require may_approve: MayApprove(actor, org, book)
    require approver_is_not_proposer: actor != proposer
    require instruction_not_yet_approved: not InstructionApproved(instruction: instruction, ..)
    admit InstructionApproved(org, instruction, actor)

-- Turn an open instruction down. It ends, and its room is free again.
-- The same two conditions as approving: the right, and a different
-- person. (Whoever placed it withdraws it with cancel_instruction.)
transformation reject_instruction(org, instruction):
    bind instruction_is_open: InstructionOpen(org: org, book: book, instruction: instruction, ..)
    bind instruction_was_placed: Instruction(org: org, instruction: instruction, proposer: proposer, ..)
    require may_approve: MayApprove(actor, org, book)
    require approver_is_not_proposer: actor != proposer
    retract InstructionOpen(org: org, instruction: instruction, ..)
    admit InstructionEnded(org, instruction, #rejected)

-- Record every instruction in a book that has run out of time. One act
-- visits every open instruction in the book and lapses those whose
-- `valid_until` has passed; the rest are left alone. Refused when none
-- has passed, so a sweep that finds nothing writes nothing. The room an
-- instruction held is already free once its time passes (see
-- held_in_hour); this act puts the ending on the record. Whoever may
-- instruct in the book may run it, and so may the reconciling service,
-- which runs it on a schedule.
transformation lapse_overdue_instructions(org, book):
    require may_lapse: MayInstruct(actor, org, book) or MayRecordFills(actor, org)
    let now = decided_at
    require something_has_lapsed: exists overdue: InstructionOpen(org: org, book: book, instruction: overdue, valid_until: deadline, ..) and deadline strictly_before now
    for InstructionOpen(org: org, book: book, instruction: i, valid_until: until, ..):
        if until strictly_before now:
            retract InstructionOpen(org: org, instruction: i, ..)
            admit InstructionEnded(org, i, #lapsed)

-- Record a fill the market reported, and book the trade it is. A fill
-- has happened, so it is booked even when it breaks a rule, and the
-- breach is recorded with it in the same act. Ordinary capture stays
-- exactly as strict: only this act, by a narrow right, books past a
-- limit.
-- The book, market, side and delivery window are the instruction's.
-- What the instruction still holds goes down by the fill. When nothing
-- is left it ends as filled.
-- A fill within what the instruction held cannot break the limit: that
-- room was held for it. Each of these is booked and recorded as a breach
-- (see Breach): the cap truly broken in some hour, judged as for a
-- trade; no approval where the book requires one; the instruction
-- already ended; a worse price than its limit price (higher for a buy,
-- lower for a sale); more than it still held.
transformation record_fill(org, instruction, execution, trade, counterparty, version, quantity, price, trade_date):
    require may_record_fills: MayRecordFills(actor, org)
    bind instruction_was_placed: Instruction(org: org, book: book, instruction: instruction, market: market, direction: direction, limit_price: limit_price, delivery_start: delivery_start, delivery_end: delivery_end, valid_until: valid_until, ..)
    require execution_is_new: not InstructionFill(org: org, execution: execution, ..)
    require trade_is_new: not TradeCaptured(trade: trade, ..)
    require terms_version_is_new: not TradeTerms(version: version, ..)
    let left = value InstructionOpen(org: org, instruction: instruction, remaining: _, ..) default 0 MW
    let still_held = left - quantity
    if quantity < left:
        retract InstructionOpen(org: org, instruction: instruction, ..)
        admit InstructionOpen(org, book, instruction, direction, still_held, delivery_start, delivery_end, valid_until)
    else:
        if InstructionOpen(org: org, instruction: instruction, ..):
            retract InstructionOpen(org: org, instruction: instruction, ..)
            admit InstructionEnded(org, instruction, #filled)
            if quantity > left:
                admit Breach(org, book, trade, #overfill)
        else:
            admit Breach(org, book, trade, #after_end)
    if (direction = #buy and price > limit_price) or (direction = #sell and price < limit_price):
        admit Breach(org, book, trade, #price)
    let signed = if(direction = #buy, quantity, 0 MW - quantity)
    if exists cap: PositionLimit(org: org, book: book, cap: cap) and not (keeps_to_cap(org, book, trade_date, trade, signed, delivery_start, delivery_end, cap) and shrinks_where_breached(org, book, trade_date, trade, signed, delivery_start, delivery_end, cap)):
        admit Breach(org, book, trade, #limit)
    if ApprovalRequired(org: org, book: book) and not InstructionApproved(org: org, instruction: instruction, ..):
        admit Breach(org, book, trade, #unapproved)
    admit InstructionFill(org, instruction, execution, trade)
    admit TradeCaptured(org, book, trade, counterparty, market, direction)
    admit TradeTerms(org, trade, version, quantity, price, delivery_start, delivery_end, trade_date)

-- Answer a breach: who looked at it, what they decided and why. The
-- answer comes from someone with the right, and never from the trader
-- who placed the instruction behind the fill: nobody signs off their
-- own breach.
transformation answer_breach(org, trade, kind, decision, note):
    require may_answer_breach: MayAnswerBreach(actor, org)
    require breach_was_recorded: Breach(org: org, trade: trade, kind: kind, ..)
    require breach_not_yet_answered: not BreachAnswered(org: org, trade: trade, kind: kind, ..)
    bind breach_came_from_a_fill: InstructionFill(org: org, instruction: instruction, trade: trade, ..)
    bind fill_was_instructed: Instruction(org: org, instruction: instruction, proposer: trader, ..)
    require answerer_is_not_the_trader: actor != trader
    admit BreachAnswered(org, trade, kind, actor, decision, note)

-- Register the first curve for a market and date, and make it official.
-- If one is already official, this is refused: use correct_curve.
transformation register_curve(org, market, as_of, version, payload_hash):
    require may_register_curve: MayRegisterCurve(actor, org, market)
    require market_has_a_currency: MarketCurrency(org: org, market: market, ..)
    require curve_version_is_new: not CurveRegistered(version: version, ..)
    require no_official_curve_yet: not OfficialCurve(org: org, market: market, as_of: as_of, ..)
    admit CurveRegistered(org, market, as_of, version, payload_hash)
    admit OfficialCurve(org, market, as_of, version)

-- Correct the official curve. The new version is registered, linked to
-- the one it replaces, and becomes official. The old version and every
-- mark made against it stay on the record.
transformation correct_curve(org, market, as_of, prior_version, new_version, payload_hash):
    require may_register_curve: MayRegisterCurve(actor, org, market)
    require prior_curve_is_official: OfficialCurve(org, market, as_of, prior_version)
    require prior_curve_is_the_latest: not CurveSupersedes(prior_version: prior_version, ..)
    require curve_version_is_new: not CurveRegistered(version: new_version, ..)
    admit CurveRegistered(org, market, as_of, new_version, payload_hash)
    admit CurveSupersedes(new_version, prior_version)
    retract OfficialCurve(org, market, as_of, prior_version)
    admit OfficialCurve(org, market, as_of, new_version)

-- Record a mark-to-market. The number is computed outside the ledger.
-- The ledger checks what it was computed from: the curve version must
-- be the official one for the trade's market on the organisation's
-- business date, and the terms version must be the one in force on that
-- date. A mark needs a business date to belong to. A mark against
-- a stale curve or the wrong terms cannot be recorded.
-- The mark is in the currency of the trade's market. The ledger looks
-- that up itself and records it beside the number, so a caller cannot
-- state another.
-- The patterns of a gate share one `require` because a name bound in
-- one pattern (`market`, `as_of`) is only visible within that `require`.
transformation admit_valuation(org, book, trade, curve_version, terms_version, mtm):
    require may_value_trade: MayValueTrade(actor, org, book)
    bind business_date_is_set: BusinessDate(org: org, date: business_date)
    require valued_against_official_curve_and_terms_in_force: TradeCaptured(org: org, book: book, trade: trade, market: market, ..)
    and OfficialCurve(org: org, market: market, as_of: business_date, version: curve_version)
    and trade_terms_in_force_on(trade: trade, as_of: business_date, org: org, version: terms_version, ..)
    bind trade_is_captured: TradeCaptured(org: org, book: book, trade: trade, market: traded_in, ..)
    bind market_has_a_currency: MarketCurrency(org: org, market: traded_in, currency: currency)
    require valuation_is_new: not TradeValued(trade: trade, curve_version: curve_version, terms_version: terms_version, ..)
    admit TradeValued(org, book, trade, curve_version, terms_version, mtm, currency)
